WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.868 --> 00:00:06.037
Alright, I'd like you to take a moment.

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.806
Think about a time when you
were recently sick.

00:00:09.573 --> 00:00:12.109
Try specifically to think of a time when,

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:14.345
even though you weren't
feeling all that great,

00:00:14.378 --> 00:00:17.181
you still felt well enough
to get up out of bed,

00:00:17.214 --> 00:00:18.649
go about your day.

00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:23.387
OK, what was your mood like?

00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:27.124
Did you feel a little sad or depressed?

00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:31.962
What types of social interactions
did you want to have?

00:00:32.596 --> 00:00:36.567
Would you have wanted to go
to a cocktail party full of strangers

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:38.302
or out on a first date?

00:00:39.336 --> 00:00:41.005
I'm a psychology professor

00:00:41.005 --> 00:00:43.607
at the University of North
Carolina at Chapel Hill,

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:45.476
and for the past 15 years,

00:00:45.509 --> 00:00:49.814
I've been studying the interconnections
between physiological changes

00:00:49.814 --> 00:00:52.650
happening in the body during sickness

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.319
and psychological and social well-being.

00:00:56.287 --> 00:01:01.659
Specifically my lab studies
social psychoneuroimmunology,

00:01:01.692 --> 00:01:03.461
which is a mouthful to say,

00:01:03.494 --> 00:01:05.663
and is a field of research
that's dedicated

00:01:05.663 --> 00:01:09.967
to discovering interactions between
our social experiences,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.404
psychological processes
and the immune system.

00:01:14.505 --> 00:01:17.007
Today, I want to tell you
about some research showing

00:01:17.041 --> 00:01:21.212
that the same physiological changes
happening in your body

00:01:21.245 --> 00:01:25.015
that cause the physical symptoms
you have when you're sick,

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.286
are also shaping your mood
and your social behavior.

00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:32.656
In other words, changes
in the immune system

00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:36.227
can signal to the brain
to cause us to think,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.696
feel and act differently.

00:01:39.797 --> 00:01:45.035
And not only that, but also our social
experiences can cause changes

00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:46.804
in our immune systems.

00:01:47.438 --> 00:01:51.142
So purely psychological things
happening in our brains

00:01:51.142 --> 00:01:55.079
can cause the immune system
to ramp up or ramp down.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.850
Because of that, we can get caught
in these vicious cycles

00:01:59.884 --> 00:02:04.889
where our psychological experiences
can cause changes in our immune system,

00:02:04.922 --> 00:02:07.858
and those immunological shifts
can cause changes

00:02:07.892 --> 00:02:10.094
to our psychological experiences.

00:02:11.595 --> 00:02:13.430
Now the component of the immune system

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.101
that most research in psycho-
neuroimmunology focuses on

00:02:17.101 --> 00:02:20.704
is the inflammatory response
or inflammation.

00:02:21.672 --> 00:02:24.241
The inflammatory response
is your immune system's

00:02:24.275 --> 00:02:28.212
first line of defense
against injury or infection,

00:02:28.245 --> 00:02:30.114
and it's coordinated by these molecules

00:02:30.147 --> 00:02:33.317
that are called
pro-inflammatory cytokines.

00:02:34.118 --> 00:02:37.221
You can think of cytokines
as sort of the chemical messengers

00:02:37.254 --> 00:02:38.956
of the immune system.

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:42.693
So they're out there right now,
swimming through your bloodstream.

00:02:42.726 --> 00:02:47.264
And if an immune cell finds
something weird or out of the ordinary,

00:02:47.298 --> 00:02:50.601
they'll tell those cytokines
to signal to other immune cells

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:52.403
to come and check it out.

00:02:53.804 --> 00:02:56.373
So if you think about a time
you've had a paper cut,

00:02:56.407 --> 00:02:59.643
you may have noticed
that the area around the cut swells,

00:02:59.677 --> 00:03:02.012
it turns red, it heats up.

00:03:02.613 --> 00:03:05.849
That's the inflammatory
response in action.

00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:10.287
And those symptoms are caused
by your cytokines doing their job,

00:03:10.321 --> 00:03:14.725
sending out signals to other immune
cells to come and heal the cut.

00:03:15.392 --> 00:03:19.530
The same thing happens
if your immune cells find a virus

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:21.432
or a bacteria in the body.

00:03:21.765 --> 00:03:25.402
They send out cytokines
to signal to other immune cells

00:03:25.436 --> 00:03:28.138
to come and try
and eliminate the pathogen.

00:03:30.341 --> 00:03:33.210
Now in doing this, cytokines cause

00:03:33.244 --> 00:03:36.747
the physical symptoms
we commonly have when we're sick.

00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:41.218
Things like fever
and achiness and fatigue.

00:03:41.785 --> 00:03:45.723
So even though we usually think of those
symptoms as being caused by a virus

00:03:45.756 --> 00:03:47.691
or a bacteria itself,

00:03:47.725 --> 00:03:50.561
they're actually caused
by our own immune systems

00:03:50.594 --> 00:03:53.731
activating to try
to eliminate the pathogen.

00:03:55.399 --> 00:03:58.969
But in addition to those
physical symptoms,

00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:00.671
decades of research,

00:04:00.704 --> 00:04:03.007
in both animals and humans,

00:04:03.007 --> 00:04:08.412
clearly shows that cytokines
also cause changes to our mood

00:04:08.445 --> 00:04:10.481
and to our social behavior.

00:04:11.115 --> 00:04:14.652
So inflammation in the body
can signal to the brain

00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:17.521
to cause us to feel down,

00:04:17.521 --> 00:04:20.524
depressed and even hopeless.

00:04:21.525 --> 00:04:26.230
Inflammation can also make us want
to socially withdraw from other people

00:04:26.263 --> 00:04:30.401
to avoid interacting with individuals
in our social networks.

00:04:31.168 --> 00:04:35.105
So this research shows the powerful
influence that the immune system can have

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:37.875
on our mood and on our social behavior.

00:04:38.842 --> 00:04:41.211
Changes in inflammation in the body

00:04:41.211 --> 00:04:46.884
can signal to the brain to cause us
to feel depressed and even lonely.

00:04:48.719 --> 00:04:50.654
OK, so you may be wondering,

00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:53.624
why on Earth would your body do this?

00:04:54.091 --> 00:04:58.929
Why would you want your immune system
to be able to manipulate your brain

00:04:58.929 --> 00:05:03.400
and cause you to feel sad and distant
from other people in your life?

00:05:04.468 --> 00:05:07.338
While we can't know for sure
why this happens,

00:05:07.371 --> 00:05:10.474
evolutionary theory provides
some good food for thought.

00:05:11.108 --> 00:05:12.443
The fact is,

00:05:12.476 --> 00:05:16.513
revving up and running the immune
system takes a lot of energy.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.616
Getting cytokines to swim
through the bloodstream

00:05:19.616 --> 00:05:23.387
and send signals to immune
cells takes calories.

00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:26.156
And what else takes calories?

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.659
Pretty much everything.

00:05:28.692 --> 00:05:32.863
Especially things like going out
and seeking pleasurable experiences,

00:05:32.863 --> 00:05:34.932
interacting with strangers

00:05:34.932 --> 00:05:37.735
and just generally moving about the world.

00:05:38.502 --> 00:05:45.142
So the theory is that the immune system
is telling the brain to feel depressed

00:05:45.175 --> 00:05:47.511
and to withdraw from socializing

00:05:47.544 --> 00:05:51.115
because it wants you
to stay at home and rest.

00:05:52.249 --> 00:05:55.185
And if things that would
normally sound fun

00:05:55.185 --> 00:05:57.788
just don't seem all that fun,

00:05:57.788 --> 00:06:01.525
and if interacting with other
people seems exhausting

00:06:01.558 --> 00:06:04.094
and maybe even a little threatening,

00:06:04.094 --> 00:06:07.131
then we'll be less likely
to do those things

00:06:07.131 --> 00:06:09.233
and more likely to stay at home

00:06:09.233 --> 00:06:12.536
and let our immune systems
use our calories.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:18.709
Now, of course, if this becomes prolonged
and inflammation is elevated over weeks

00:06:18.709 --> 00:06:20.477
or even years,

00:06:20.511 --> 00:06:23.847
that could have really terrible
impacts on our well-being.

00:06:24.548 --> 00:06:26.016
But in the short term,

00:06:26.049 --> 00:06:29.920
we think of this immune-to-brain
signaling as adaptive.

00:06:30.521 --> 00:06:34.992
Your immune system is basically good
at knowing when it would be a good idea

00:06:35.025 --> 00:06:39.129
for you to be out interacting
with the world and the people in it,

00:06:39.163 --> 00:06:41.965
and when it would be better
to just stay at home.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.905
But it turns out, the influence
of inflammation on our social lives

00:06:47.905 --> 00:06:52.342
isn't as simple as always
making us feel more disconnected

00:06:52.376 --> 00:06:54.044
and socially withdrawn.

00:06:54.878 --> 00:06:56.647
One of the most important discoveries

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.683
that we've made in this area
of research recently

00:06:59.683 --> 00:07:03.720
is that inflammation might actually
make us more motivated

00:07:03.754 --> 00:07:06.857
to seek some social interactions,

00:07:06.857 --> 00:07:10.527
specifically those with the people
who we're closest to.

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:15.232
So it’s not that inflammation
makes us less social across the board,

00:07:15.532 --> 00:07:20.704
it may just make us more selective
about who we want to socialize with.

00:07:21.738 --> 00:07:25.242
To discover this, my colleagues
and I ran an experiment.

00:07:26.009 --> 00:07:27.911
We asked participants in our study

00:07:27.911 --> 00:07:31.482
to send us digital photographs
of someone who they could go to

00:07:31.482 --> 00:07:33.951
if they needed help or support.

00:07:34.785 --> 00:07:39.156
As you would expect, people sent us
photos of their romantic partners,

00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:41.625
their closest friends and their family.

00:07:42.726 --> 00:07:47.297
Then we had our participants view
those images of their support figure

00:07:47.331 --> 00:07:50.701
while we tracked their brain activity
using MRI scanning.

00:07:51.401 --> 00:07:54.204
Now, critically, prior to the MRI scans,

00:07:54.238 --> 00:07:58.075
we exposed a random half
of the participants in our study

00:07:58.108 --> 00:08:03.013
to a bacteria which cause levels
of inflammation in the body to increase.

00:08:03.614 --> 00:08:07.351
The other half just had normal,
low levels of inflammation.

00:08:08.318 --> 00:08:13.023
And what we found is that for the people
who we exposed to that bacteria,

00:08:13.056 --> 00:08:15.993
who had lots of cytokines in their body,

00:08:16.026 --> 00:08:21.532
they actually showed more activity
in a reward-related region of the brain,

00:08:21.565 --> 00:08:23.367
called the ventral striatum,

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.903
when they viewed images
of their support figures

00:08:25.936 --> 00:08:29.039
relative to the people who had
low levels of inflammation.

00:08:30.073 --> 00:08:31.275
So what this suggests

00:08:31.308 --> 00:08:36.113
is that while inflammation might make
social interactions with strangers

00:08:36.146 --> 00:08:38.181
seem less rewarding,

00:08:38.181 --> 00:08:43.220
it doesn't necessarily make all social
interactions seem less rewarding.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.492
In fact, inflammation may make us
more motivated to seek interactions

00:08:48.525 --> 00:08:51.828
with people who could provide us
with comfort or care.

00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:55.799
Those who could be a shortcut
to chicken soup.

00:08:57.868 --> 00:08:59.703
But of course, it's not every day

00:08:59.736 --> 00:09:03.006
that we come into contact
with a virus or a bacteria,

00:09:03.006 --> 00:09:04.675
or that we have a wound

00:09:04.675 --> 00:09:07.844
that causes our levels
of inflammation to increase.

00:09:08.412 --> 00:09:12.516
So how much of a day-to-day
influence on our social lives

00:09:12.549 --> 00:09:15.118
does the immune system actually have?

00:09:16.086 --> 00:09:21.058
Research shows that the answer
is a lot more than you might think.

00:09:21.959 --> 00:09:25.062
That's because one
of the most robust findings

00:09:25.095 --> 00:09:27.998
in all of psychoneuroimmunology research

00:09:27.998 --> 00:09:31.335
is that stress causes inflammation.

00:09:32.536 --> 00:09:35.806
So purely psychological
things happening in the brain

00:09:35.839 --> 00:09:39.176
can cause increases
in cytokines in the body.

00:09:39.776 --> 00:09:41.645
This is true across species,

00:09:41.678 --> 00:09:44.815
from rodents to primates to humans.

00:09:45.782 --> 00:09:48.218
In my lab and in others across the globe,

00:09:48.251 --> 00:09:51.355
we study the effects of stress
on inflammation

00:09:51.355 --> 00:09:55.325
by asking people to come
into the lab and give a ten-minute,

00:09:55.325 --> 00:09:56.927
off-the-cuff speech

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.130
about why they'd be a good
candidate for their dream job.

00:10:00.464 --> 00:10:04.601
And then they have to do challenging
mental math out loud.

00:10:04.635 --> 00:10:08.505
And they do this all in front of a panel
of experts who's judging them.

00:10:09.606 --> 00:10:12.209
Yeah, people report
that this is stressful.

00:10:12.943 --> 00:10:14.811
And critically,

00:10:14.845 --> 00:10:18.749
in response to this psychologically
stressful experience,

00:10:18.782 --> 00:10:24.021
we see that levels of cytokines
in the body have increased.

00:10:25.222 --> 00:10:30.227
Let's take a second and really appreciate
that remarkable finding.

00:10:31.728 --> 00:10:35.799
In the absence of anything physically
wrong with the body --

00:10:35.799 --> 00:10:40.070
no viruses, no bacteria, no wounds --

00:10:40.103 --> 00:10:42.539
a purely psychological stressor

00:10:42.572 --> 00:10:45.542
is causing the inflammatory
response to activate.

00:10:46.743 --> 00:10:50.947
So it's not just that our immune systems
are sending signals to our brain,

00:10:50.981 --> 00:10:55.152
but also our brains are sending signals
to our immune systems

00:10:55.152 --> 00:10:59.022
telling them to activate in response
to stress that we encounter.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:00.991
OK, so now that you know

00:11:01.024 --> 00:11:04.628
that your immune system
has this very active social life

00:11:04.661 --> 00:11:08.865
and that your brain and your immune system
are constantly communicating

00:11:08.899 --> 00:11:13.370
in ways that could make you feel
depressed or lonely,

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.905
what should you do?

00:11:16.073 --> 00:11:19.843
For me, knowing about the social life
of the immune system

00:11:19.876 --> 00:11:21.845
has changed my life,

00:11:21.878 --> 00:11:24.614
in part because it gives me an explanation

00:11:24.614 --> 00:11:29.553
for why I might feel physically crummy
at the end of a stressful day,

00:11:29.553 --> 00:11:32.022
or why I might feel a little down

00:11:32.022 --> 00:11:35.992
and disconnected from other people
when I'm feeling physically unwell.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.695
So, for example,

00:11:38.729 --> 00:11:44.067
the experience of giving this talk today
has been really rewarding

00:11:44.067 --> 00:11:46.269
and also quite challenging.

00:11:46.269 --> 00:11:49.673
You might even say it was,
at times, stressful.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:54.311
And I'm sure you've had similar
experiences in your lives.

00:11:54.344 --> 00:11:58.348
Maybe it was a difficult task
you had to complete at work,

00:11:58.381 --> 00:12:01.818
a confrontation with a spouse
or a family member,

00:12:01.852 --> 00:12:04.321
or just some problem
that cropped up in your life

00:12:04.321 --> 00:12:06.990
that you had to quickly
figure out how to solve.

00:12:08.158 --> 00:12:10.026
Based on what I've told you here today,

00:12:10.026 --> 00:12:14.464
I hope you're predicting that in response
to these stressful experiences,

00:12:14.498 --> 00:12:18.401
we're likely to have high levels
of inflammation in our bodies.

00:12:18.802 --> 00:12:21.838
Our cytokines will
have come out in full force

00:12:21.872 --> 00:12:24.708
in response to the stress
we've experienced.

00:12:25.976 --> 00:12:27.611
And because of that,

00:12:27.644 --> 00:12:31.448
if we then feel exhausted

00:12:31.448 --> 00:12:34.151
and a bit like we've failed

00:12:34.184 --> 00:12:38.588
and we don't want to go anywhere
or see anyone or do anything,

00:12:38.588 --> 00:12:41.958
and we just want to stay at home
and relax on the couch

00:12:41.992 --> 00:12:44.961
with the people who we're closest to,

00:12:44.995 --> 00:12:46.196
we'll know why.

00:12:47.097 --> 00:12:52.102
And because we know that those symptoms
are likely just our immune systems

00:12:52.135 --> 00:12:54.437
telling our brains to feel depressed

00:12:54.471 --> 00:12:56.973
and to withdraw from social interactions

00:12:57.007 --> 00:13:01.344
so that we stay at home to rest,
relax and recuperate,

00:13:01.378 --> 00:13:03.547
we don't have to agonize

00:13:03.547 --> 00:13:06.516
over why we're maybe feeling
a little down on ourselves,

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.852
or a little distant from other people.

00:13:09.352 --> 00:13:12.255
We can rest assured that

00:13:12.255 --> 00:13:14.090
in the coming days,

00:13:14.124 --> 00:13:17.294
we'll be feeling much more
like ourselves again

00:13:17.327 --> 00:13:19.896
as our cytokine levels go back down.

00:13:21.164 --> 00:13:22.332
So in the future,

00:13:22.332 --> 00:13:24.467
when you encounter stress in your life

00:13:24.467 --> 00:13:28.872
and you feel those reins
of inflammation start to take hold,

00:13:28.905 --> 00:13:31.675
I hope you remember
what I've told you here today.

00:13:32.442 --> 00:13:36.446
And you remind yourself that those
symptoms you're experiencing,

00:13:36.479 --> 00:13:39.182
that's likely just your immune system

00:13:39.216 --> 00:13:41.084
doing its job,

00:13:41.117 --> 00:13:45.355
telling your brain to feel
depressed and withdrawn

00:13:45.388 --> 00:13:47.657
as evolution intended,

00:13:47.691 --> 00:13:50.160
to keep you healthy in the long term.

00:13:50.894 --> 00:13:52.262
Thank you very much.

00:13:52.295 --> 00:13:54.831
(Applause)

